  <section>
    <h2>Know your native soil: three Maine soil regions</h2>
    <p>Maine soils were shaped by glaciers, and the state's soil map roughly follows its geography. Knowing which region you're in tells you what you're fighting — drainage, acidity, or compaction.</p>

    <h3>Aroostook and the north: glacial loam</h3>
    <p>Aroostook County's famous potato ground is a well-drained, stone-free glacial loam — arguably the best native agricultural soil in the state. It holds moisture and nutrients well but still runs acidic (typically 5.0–5.8) and needs lime for cannabis. If you're growing in The County, your soil problem is the mildest in Maine: correct pH, add compost, and you have a genuinely good medium.</p>

    <h3>Coastal plain: sandy and fast-draining</h3>
    <p>The southern coast and midcoast (York, Cumberland, Sagadahoc counties) sit on sandy glacial outwash and marine deposits. Sandy soil drains fast and warms early — a real advantage in a short season — but it also leaches nutrients and dries out during July's dry spells. Cannabis in coastal sand needs frequent feeding, generous compost to hold water and cations, and mulch. pH typically runs 4.8–5.6.</p>

    <h3>Central and western interior: clay and hardpan</h3>
    <p>Central Maine (Kennebec, Androscoggin, Penobscot) and the western mountains carry heavy clay and glacial hardpan — the dense, gray, slow-draining layer Maine gardeners hit a foot down. Clay holds nutrients but suffocates roots, stays cold into June, and compacts. Cannabis roots need oxygen; in heavy clay they drown. This is the region where raised beds or fabric pots pay for themselves fastest, because amending clay in place takes years, not weeks.</p>
    <p class="source-note">Soil region generalizations follow USDA NRCS soil survey data for Maine. Your specific lot can differ — a former farm field, a filled lot, or a wooded slope each carries a different profile. Test before you build.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>pH testing and correction: the Maine-specific problem</h2>
    <p>Cannabis in soil absorbs nutrients optimally between pH 6.0 and 7.0, with 6.0–6.5 the sweet spot. Below about 5.5, phosphorus, calcium, and magnesium lock up — the classic symptoms are dark or purple stems, stunted growth, and yellowing that doesn't respond to feeding. Above 7.0, iron, manganese, and zinc lock up instead. Most Maine native soils sit at 4.5–5.5, squarely in lockout territory.</p>

    <h3>Test before you amend</h3>
    <p>The University of Maine Analytical Laboratory and Research Center (Orono) runs the standard soil test Maine growers rely on — roughly $15–20 for the basic panel, reporting pH, buffer pH, phosphorus, potassium, calcium, magnesium, and organic matter, with lime and fertilizer recommendations calibrated to Maine soils. Mail a composite sample (10–15 cores from the root zone, mixed, about a pint). Cheap probe meters give a directional read but drift; a $15 lab test is the best money in the whole grow.</p>

    <h3>Lime: how much and when</h3>
    <p>Dolomitic lime (calcium-magnesium carbonate) is the standard correction for Maine soils, which are usually low in both calcium and magnesium. The lab report gives you a rate based on buffer pH; a typical correction from 5.0 to 6.5 runs 5–10 lb of dolomitic lime per 100 square feet, worked into the top 6–8 inches.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Timing:</strong> lime moves slowly. Fall application before the ground freezes is ideal — freeze-thaw cycles work it in. If you're correcting in spring, apply at least 3–4 weeks before transplant and water it in.</li>
      <li><strong>Dolomitic vs. calcitic:</strong> use dolomitic unless your test shows high magnesium. Most Maine soils are magnesium-deficient, which is exactly what dolomitic supplies.</li>
      <li><strong>Don't overshoot:</strong> more lime is not better. Pushing past pH 7.0 creates the opposite lockout problem, and over-liming takes a full season to reverse.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-test yearly.</strong> Maine rainfall leaches calcium and magnesium, so pH drifts back down over time. Annual re-testing keeps you honest.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout title="pH correction is a season-long habit, not a one-time fix" type="tip">
      Maine's 40–46 inches of annual rainfall continuously pull bases out of the soil profile. A bed corrected to 6.5 this spring may read 6.0 by next. Re-test every year, top-dress a light lime application each fall, and keep a log — after two seasons you'll know your soil's drift rate and can correct on a schedule instead of guessing.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Raised beds: the reliable Maine path</h2>
    <p>A raised bed solves Maine's three soil problems at once — it replaces acidic native soil with a controlled mix, lifts the root zone above cold, saturated spring ground, and drains freely through September's wet weeks. For central-Maine clay especially, it's not a luxury; it's the difference between a crop and a compost pile.</p>

    <h3>Build notes for Maine conditions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> 4×8 ft is the standard — reachable from both sides. Depth 12–18 inches; cannabis roots will use all of it.</li>
      <li><strong>Material:</strong> untreated cedar or hemlock lasts 8–15 years in Maine weather. Avoid pressure-treated lumber rated with older copper-arsenate chemistries; modern ACQ is considered acceptable for food crops, but most growers prefer to skip treated wood for cannabis beds.</li>
      <li><strong>Frost heave:</strong> Maine's freeze-thaw cycle will lift and rack a shallow frame. Set corner posts 18–24 inches into the ground, or accept that a frame sitting on grade may need re-squaring each spring. A bed anchored to driven stakes survives heave.</li>
      <li><strong>Bottom:</strong> leave it open to native soil (with hardware cloth underneath against voles and moles) so excess water drains and roots can reach down. A sealed-bottom bed becomes a bathtub in September.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Filling the bed</h3>
    <p>A proven Maine bed mix by volume: roughly 40% quality topsoil or loam, 40% finished compost, and 20% aeration (perlite, rice hulls, or coarse sand). Top-dress with an organic fertilizer blend at transplant and again at first flower. One 4×8×12-inch bed holds about 32 cubic feet of mix — plan delivery or pickup accordingly; bulk loam/compost from a local yard is dramatically cheaper per cubic foot than bagged.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Fabric pots: the flexible alternative</h2>
    <p>Fabric pots (10–20 gallon) have become the default container for Maine outdoor growers, and for reasons specific to this climate:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Air-pruning:</strong> roots hit the fabric wall and branch instead of circling, producing a dense, efficient root ball.</li>
      <li><strong>Drainage:</strong> overwatering — the top killer of young cannabis — is nearly impossible in fabric. September's wet weeks drain themselves.</li>
      <li><strong>Soil temperature:</strong> fabric breathes, so the root zone stays closer to air temperature. In a cool Maine June that's a small penalty; in July it prevents the cooked-roots problem black plastic pots suffer in full sun.</li>
      <li><strong>Season flexibility:</strong> pots can move to chase sun, come under a tarp during a September deluge, or shift into a hoop house for the final weeks of finish. You can't do that with an in-ground bed.</li>
      <li><strong>Compliance and discretion:</strong> a few fabric pots tucked behind a fence are far less conspicuous than a tilled garden, and easy to relocate if your visibility situation changes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The tradeoffs: fabric dries faster than ground or raised beds (expect near-daily watering in a July dry spell), and pots need saucers or a gravel base on a deck. Use a minimum of 10 gallons for a full-season photoperiod plant — 20 gallons is better — and fill with the same loam/compost/aeration mix you'd use in a bed.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Compost, organic matter, and the living soil approach</h2>
    <p>Maine native soils typically run 2–4% organic matter; cannabis thrives at 5–10%+. Compost is how you close that gap, and Maine makes it easy: municipal drop-offs often give away finished leaf compost, and every feed store in the state carries bagged composted manure. Your own backyard pile, fed all winter, will be ready by June.</p>
    <p>The living-soil approach takes this further: instead of feeding the plant with bottled nutrients, you feed a soil food web — compost, worm castings, kelp meal, crab or lobster meal (a Maine coastal staple), rock phosphate, and a fungal-dominated inoculant — and let microbes mineralize nutrition on demand. A properly built living bed needs little more than water and a seasonal top-dress, and the microbial life suppresses root disease in ways sterile bagged soil can't. The catch is lead time: a living soil wants 4–8 weeks of "cooking" before roots go in, so build beds in April for a June transplant, or the prior fall.</p>
    <Callout title="Maine's lobster and crab meal advantage" type="info">
      Coastal Maine growers have cheap local access to crab meal, lobster meal, and kelp meal — excellent slow-release sources of nitrogen, calcium, chitin, and micronutrients. Chitin in particular feeds chitinolytic microbes and is associated with improved resistance to fungal pressure, relevant in a state where botrytis ends the season. Check local feed co-ops and fishery suppliers before ordering online.
    </Callout>
  </section>

